#{[17592186107155 "Given a linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with an associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ there are three possibilities regarding the type of object formed under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ by a two-dimensional preimage. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the three-sided polygon $\\mathcal P$ with vertices $(0,1)$, $(2,0)$ and $(2,1),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/97/97689c7aaf7f6d42314fd01cb36b557df8081880659bcea08905e6c57f358ac1.png)\n\nLet's describe the three possible types of object formed by the image of $\\mathcal P{:}$\n\n- Case where $T$ is non-singular:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$T =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nWe can easily check that $T$ is non-singular by computing its determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $\\mathcal P$ and then compute the product $TX{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a triangle.\n\n![](images/14/145753e4f69dfa67bb45f59b7e9ed8bca789c589d4c5340ce2112363fa70cd7b.png)\n\n- Case where $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular and has at least one nonzero entry:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that $T$ is singular by computing its determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of  $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment.\n\n![](images/46/46d387dbf6678d324135dbbb3439688c0bdbf9f9567baef56f34aa4aa9ee22e3.png)\n\n- Case where $T$ is the zero matrix:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation represented by the zero matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nClearly, $T$ is singular. Now, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are all $(0,0)$, the origin $O$ of the coordinate system. \n\n![](images/db/dbb9fce92da1f231a31e110f6cabb6faaf1bb43961a6c1de012c7c302c7af928.png)" #edb/ref 13194139536169 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186107156 "In general, for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system." #edb/ref 13194139533439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107157 "Suppose we are given a point $P$ with position vector $\\mathbf{p},$ and another vector $\\mathbf{v}.$ \n\n![](images/b1/b1bb8d0cbe5dcbb24e224e8efc77e32db7a1657b245666aebd7b18107ee41149.png)\n\nThe **vector equation** of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=p+tv,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is often called the **direction vector** of the line, while the vector $\\mathbf{r}$ denotes the position vector of an arbitrary point on the line.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe vector equation of the line  that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535467 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186107158 "There is a geometric intuition behind the formula $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/93/93b062a23c613184e7cdf725852401168b609a32056a5efcd24ceca2703d81d2.png)\n\nTo get to any point $A(x,y,z)$ on the desired line, starting from the origin $O,$ we can\n\n- first travel along the vector $\\mathbf{p}$ to get to the point $P$ on the line, and\n\n- then travel along the vector $t \\mathbf{v}$ to get to $A.$ (The scalar $t$ represents how far we travel forward or backward along the line in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$)" #edb/ref 13194139533441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107159 "![](images/44/443bbe1d201f1919fa2fb7ce3b632715cdc0e8dc77ff94bdc89f2cff7402b772.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to find a vector equation of the line *segment* whose endpoints are $P(3,-1,7)$ and $Q(3,4,4).$ \n\nTo do this, we need to find the vector equation of the corresponding line and then restrict the domain of the parameter $t.$\n\nFirst, we need to find a direction vector parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5t-1 \\\\ 7-3t\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nThis equation describes every point on the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}.$ \n\nTo restrict this to points on the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ only, we set $t\\in [0,1].$ Thus, the vector equation of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5t-1 \\\\ 7-3t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWriting the equation of a line segment in this way is sometimes called **parametrizing** the line segment since each point on the segment corresponds to a single parameter $t.$\n\n- Notice that $t=0$ gives us the position vector of $P{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(0) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5(0)-1 \\\\ 7-3(0)\\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} = \\mathbf p\n$$\n\n- Similarly, $t=1$ gives us the position vector of $Q{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(1) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5(1)-1 \\\\ 7-3(1)\\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}  = \\mathbf q\n$$\n\nSince increasing $t$ moves our vector $\\mathbf r (t)$ from $P$ to $Q,$ we say that the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is **traversed** *from* the point $P$ *to* the point $Q$ under this parametrization." #edb/ref 13194139536169 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186107160 "![](images/24/24c4c952103f245cd4dc32e34d5dd2363478f5400ddecfbe2503130135c01946.png)\n\nConsider the regular pentagon above. What is the measure of each interior angle?\n\nRecall that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).$ \n\nAll interior angles in a regular polygon are congruent. So, to calculate the measure of each interior angle, we find the sum of the interior angles and divide by the number of angles.\n\nA pentagon has $n=5$ sides. Therefore, the sum of its interior angles is\n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\times (5 - 2) = 540^\\circ.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the measure of each interior angle is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{540^\\circ}{5} = 108^\\circ,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a74a23a8dd760a31a0888eb694e36978c4767fd32bca6fae4fe8fe380c85e2e1.png)" #edb/ref 13194139535457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107161 "A **regular polygon** is a polygon where all sides are congruent (have the same length), and all interior angles are congruent (have the same measure).\n\nSome examples of regular polygons are shown below.\n\n![](images/d3/d33f23d3d3e301e336d7f70f37e88eee0f605c31b098969805d7c6346e34470e.png)\n\nSome examples of polygons that are **NOT** regular are as follows:\n\n- The rectangle below is **NOT** regular since the sides are not all congruent.\n\n![](images/6d/6d0c6eaee1ae1932d3bd44013c0b8aca977cbe0d98242d5c10aaa24d4f281a77.png)\n\n- The rhombus below is **NOT** regular since the interior angles are not all congruent.\n\n![](images/ce/ce2b908945db56934f08dc0da47f6c708f5220bce3a80f5147362c8b915c5613.png)\n\n- A $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle is **NOT** regular since neither the sides nor the angles are all congruent.\n\n![](images/bf/bfe00eb1d1374252fd12f6c74eff7c052021a7305ce10027efc8869f561e3ef5.png)" #edb/ref 13194139533357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107162 "Consider the polygon $ABCD$, where the side $\\overline{AB}$ is extended beyond the vertex $B.$\n\n![](images/fa/fa23c036920e2c506c38f1e7be8a498c0a349ef145e8c444201665345bc648e4.png)\n\nWe call $\\color{blue}\\angle{CBE}$ the **exterior angle** of the vertex $B$.\n\nGiven a polygon's vertex, the interior and exterior angles are supplementary. So, for the vertex $B,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\color{YellowOrange}m\\angle{ABC}}_{\\large\\text{Interior}} \\:+\\: \\underbrace{\\color{blue}m\\angle{CBE}}_{\\large\\text{Exterior}} = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe have the following theorem:\n\nThe measures of the exterior angles of any simple polygon sum to $360^\\circ.$\n\nWe'll show why this theorem is true at the end of the lesson.\n\nLet's label the exterior angles of our polygon below:\n\n![](images/ac/acc0ac90f27a78e83b6b14651f61ddfe6a87afa56b6e51a1af57f5e74c31689f.png)\n\nAccording to our theorem, these four angles sum to $360^\\circ.$\n\n$$\na+b+c+d = 360^\\circ\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107163 "Why do the exterior angles of a polygon always sum to $360^\\circ?$\n\nSuppose we have an $n$-sided polygon. Then, we have the following:\n\n- There are $n$ pairs of interior-exterior angles, each pair being supplementary (i.e., their measures add to $180^\\circ).$\n\n- Therefore, the sum of the measures of *all* interior-exterior pairs equals ${\\color{blue}n\\cdot 180^\\circ}.$\n\n- In a previous lesson, we saw that the sum of the *interior* angles equals ${\\color{red}{(n-2)\\cdot 180^\\circ}}.$\n\nTherefore, to get the sum of the *exterior* angles, we subtract ${\\color{red}{(n-2)\\cdot 180^\\circ}}$ from ${\\color{blue}n\\cdot 180^\\circ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Sum of Exterior Angles} & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘}-(n-2)\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘}-n\\cdot {180}^{∘}+2\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\cancel{n\\cdot {180}^{∘}}-\\cancel{n\\cdot {180}^{∘}}+2\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis proves that the sum of the exterior angles of a (simple) polygon equals $360^\\circ.$" #edb/ref 13194139535456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107164 "To find the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence given two arbitrary terms, we must usually apply the following:\n\n- The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n$$\n\nwhere $a_n$ is the $n$th term, $a_1$ is the first term, and $d$ is the common difference.\n\n- We can find the common difference $d$ of an arithmetic sequence given two arbitrary terms using\n\n$$\nd = \\frac{a_m-a_k}{m-k}\n$$\n\nwhere $a_m$ is the $m$th term, and $a_k$ is the $k$th term.\n\nLet's see some examples." #edb/ref 13194139535762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107165 "![](images/8e/8e128a2320d2658cc61cf83a1871d09caa7e4527b684c2af9086f80a00ecc717.png)\n\nConsider the regular hexagon above. What is the measure of each *exterior* angle?\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The exterior angles in a regular polygon are all congruent.\n\n- The sum of the exterior angles of *any* convex polygon equals $360^\\circ.$\n\nSo, to find the measure of the exterior angles of a regular polygon, we divide $360^\\circ$ by the number of sides.\n\nSince a regular hexagon has $n=6$ sides, the measure of each exterior angle is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{6} = 60^\\circ,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f4538d9e2e09715fa3aa2035cd8b22cdc326b0ac02326551d193f26f1946473.png)" #edb/ref 13194139535457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107166 "The **vector projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the portion that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends along $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that $|\\mathbf{a}|=2,$ $|\\mathbf{b}|=3,$ and the angle between the vectors is $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\n![](images/a2/a28278cf12885ab7f845407ac2fa7f4ffa00dbe30849a3f8ff41f02e134ed3a0.png)\n\nThe starting point of the vector projection is $O,$ and the ending point will be the point $P$ that lies on the line containing $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0b267fb009e79eb16f86be42edc2c835808b28aa3eaf1c7e857300224ee01ec.png)\n\nThe vector $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ is called the **vector projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$ It is denoted as $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a},$ and it is computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =OP\\cdot u \\\\  & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} = |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta$ is the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{|\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}b \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139534863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107167 "We have been using the following formula for the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} =\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can write an alternative formula using the formula for the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the original formula for the vector projection, and also substituting $\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{| \\, \\mathbf{b} \\, |},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}\\cdot \\frac{b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,{|}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107168 "Notice that if two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular, then by projecting $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or $\\mathbf{b}$ onto $\\mathbf{a}$), we obtain the zero vector.\n\nFor example, the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto a perpendicular vector $\\mathbf{b}$ looks as follows:\n\n![](images/91/9163eda36b752f1e36195b8b5a1d40bf545ec21a3988f5480d6ddcbb926c00ea.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following fact:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals the zero vector if and only if the vectors are perpendicular.*" #edb/ref 13194139534863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107169 "Whenever we have a fraction that contains a square root in the denominator, such as $\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{2}},$ we often want to find an equivalent fraction that has no square root in the denominator.\n\nTo find this equivalent fraction, all we need to do is multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the square root in the denominator. This process is known as **rationalizing the denominator**.\n\nFor example, for the fraction $\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{2}},$ we can remove the square root from the denominator by multiplying both the numerator and denominator by the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when we rationalize the denominator in the fraction  $\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{2}},$ we get the equivalent fraction $\\dfrac{3 \\sqrt{2} }{ 2 }$ that has no square root in the denominator." #edb/ref 13194139535245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107170 "We've made use of the following theorem throughout this lesson. \n\n*The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).$*\n\nThis formula works because any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles using diagonals that meet at one fixed vertex of the polygon. \n\nLet's illustrate this for a few different polygons.\n\n| ![](images/20/20aae267ba75bb418e509d548b1519af47b4cf9464d7f8400369c8e77b013aae.png) | **Quadrilateral** \n\nNumber of vertices: $4$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $4 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}} = 360^\\circ$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](images/20/20551ad9f4661402aef9e501bf9d4c841e671b4277fae78f85d9e9ada9830592.png) | **Pentagon** \n\nNumber of vertices: $5$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $5 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{3}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}} = 540^\\circ$ |\n| ![](images/de/de8ec9be742a40384891de3a682a151e817f6c24d624e0ef505fb98ce6a1e60d.png) | **Hexagon** \n\nNumber of vertices: $6$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $6 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{4}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{4}} = 720^\\circ$ |\n\nThe sum of the interior angles of each polygon is the same as the sum of the interior angles of the corresponding triangles. \n\nEach triangle has interior angles that sum to $180^\\circ,$ and any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles in the manner shown above. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles in an $n$-sided polygon is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107171 "![](images/3c/3c66bb7323dbf549baf3f3e4791e675cc21b057bd650edbe49531e35ab51d499.png)\n\nWe'll now derive the formula for the area of a trapezoid. Consider a trapezoid with bases of length $a, b$, and height $h$, as shown above.\n\nLet's now make another copy of the figure, rotate it $180^\\circ$, and place it next to the original.\n\n![](images/8c/8c6edc6c3e8f5a68f27fca43685c53ce53d61c1c3ba3c57174cd8aaa77223a07.png)\n\nWe now have a parallelogram with a base of length $a+b$ and a height of $h$. The area of this parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area of Parallelogram} = \\textrm{base} \\times \\textrm{height} = (a+b) \\times h\n$$\n\nTo find the area of the original trapezoid, we divide the above value in half:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area of Trapezoid} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Area of Parallelogram}}{2} = \\dfrac{(a+b) \\cdot h}{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107172 "![](images/13/138a83cb19d4fd454e839deef508ad2e763b3f41ec7ed8758991fc785c7f61c6.png)\n\nWe'll now derive the formula for the area of a parallelogram. Consider a parallelogram with base $b$ and height $h$, as shown above.\n\nLet's now cut the small triangle along the height on the right-hand side and place it on the other side of the original figure.\n\n![](images/cf/cfa2150c3e49a4f5704fd51240c753a9989c326ff734e0237802ec532ca7e8f4.png)\n\nWe now have a rectangle with a base of length $b$ and a height of $h$. The area of this rectangle equals the area of our parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area of Parallelogram} = \\textrm{Area of Rectangle} = \\textrm{base} \\times \\textrm{height} = b \\times h\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107173 "Consider an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty, \\infty),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent." #edb/ref 13194139533441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107174 "The **scalar projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the distance that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\n![](images/a2/a28278cf12885ab7f845407ac2fa7f4ffa00dbe30849a3f8ff41f02e134ed3a0.png)\n\nTo illustrate the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b},$ consider the point $P$ on the line that contains $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd37bdb2a97b4125fb360990d3c4a36b48bcb50433de033a154799db00e474dd.png)\n\nThe length $OP$ is called the **scalar projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or the **component** of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b}$). It is denoted as $\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a},$ and we can compute it using cosine in $\\triangle OPA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =OP \\\\  & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula is the general definition of the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107175 "We have been using the following formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nHowever, sometimes we are not given the angle between two vectors. In such cases, we can solve for $\\cos \\theta$ in terms of the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our original formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =|\\,a\\,|\\cdot \\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107176 "Notice that if two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular, then the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or $\\mathbf{b}$ onto $\\mathbf{a}$) is equal to $0.$\n\nFor example, consider the following illustration. Notice that, since $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ doesn't extend in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}$ at all. So, the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is $0.$\n\n![](images/ef/efb79d8645a5df54d76c0199335aad61b258e400a98dc485ccd8b3abc8e0ba8d.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following fact:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals $0$ if and only if the vectors are perpendicular.*" #edb/ref 13194139533461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107177 "The scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ can be negative. This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c19648ea2df1935e89e10df2f7f5ab33ad537a5fdb52bb97c3555c9a0365701e.png)\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ where it is known that \n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2, \\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ.\n$$\n\nNotice that the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors is obtuse. \n\nComputing the scalar projection of $\\mathbf a$ onto $\\mathbf b$ using the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if $90^\\circ < \\theta \\leq 180^\\circ,$ then $\\cos\\theta <0$ and the scalar projection will be negative." #edb/ref 13194139535213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107178 "The shape below consists of $6$ line segments defined by the $6$ points $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$, and $F.$ These line segments form a loop. \n\n![](images/25/259bfd166a7870bd1a057b28d4b3324f3b98d05222596f1426afa01d6573e3d8.png)\n\nA chain of straight line segments that form a loop is called a **polygon**.\n\nThe line segments are the **sides** of the polygon, and the endpoints of the sides are the **vertices**.\n\nFor this polygon:\n\n- The $6$ vertices are $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$, and $F.$\n\n- The $6$ sides are $\\overline{AB}$, $\\overline{BC}$, $\\overline{CD}$, $\\overline{DE}$, $\\overline{EF}$, and $\\overline{AF}.$\n\nTo denote a particular polygon, we start at one vertex and loop around the polygon, reading off the vertices in order. So, the polygon above can be denoted $ABCDEF.$\n\nThe singular form of the word *vertices* is **vertex**. So, $A$ is a vertex, $B$ is a vertex, etc." #edb/ref 13194139534885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107179 "The name of a polygon is determined by the number of sides it has. \n\nThe polygon below has $6$ sides. A six-sided polygon is called a **hexagon**.\n\n![](images/25/259bfd166a7870bd1a057b28d4b3324f3b98d05222596f1426afa01d6573e3d8.png)\n\nThe names of the polygons with up to ten sides are given below:\n\n| Number of sides | Name |\n| --- | --- |\n| $3$ | Triangle |\n| $4$ | Quadrilateral |\n| $5$ | Pentagon |\n| $6$ | Hexagon |\n| $7$ | Heptagon |\n| $8$ | Octagon |\n| $9$ | Nonagon |\n| $10$ | Decagon |" #edb/ref 13194139534885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107180 "Consider the pentagon $ABCDE,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b11b509e881cd813ff6879a0a6fa9559d51ffcbc5da4c150a0eaf0a9ee82a159.png)\n\nWe have the following definitions: \n\n- Two *sides* are **adjacent** (or **consecutive**) if they have a common vertex.\n\nFor example, $\\color[rgb]{0.753,0.494,0.643}\\overline{BC}$ is adjacent to $\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}\\overline{AB}$ *and* ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}\\overline{CD}}.$\n\n![](images/76/760a9cbccd117214d4e93392cdae17bf117497501b05f7b2e25c6c8cb5cd43e1.png)\n\n- Two *vertices* are **adjacent** if there is a side joining them. \n\nFor example, the vertex $E$ is adjacent to the vertices $D$ and $A$.\n\n![](images/6d/6d857920609da14fb0b31f762400c5f54e9e428717c2b55fcb17215280bbbfed.png)\n\n- A **diagonal** is a line segment that joins two non-adjacent vertices. \n\nFor example, ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{AC}}}, {\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{BD}}},$ and ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{BE}}}$ are diagonals of our pentagon.\n\n![](images/ad/adf7afaa66144a33dba7ff3732b01619bc13cac0fae59c466393d4084c748a59.png)" #edb/ref 13194139534885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107181 "When finding the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b},$ we sometimes find that the resulting projection vector points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}.$ This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/0e/0e973e6f6484a9aad863ebfe89fc1ea6356d6a50844931306c6aa2b63be18a8a.png)\n\nConsider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ where it is known that \n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2,\\qquad |\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|=3,\\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ.\n$$\n\nLet's calculate $\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}.$ \n\nFirst, notice that the angle between $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ is obtuse. This means we expect the projection vector to point in the opposite direction to that of $\\mathbf b.$\n\nNext, we compute the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the scalar projection is negative. \n\nThen, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{|\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis vector points in the direction opposite to $\\mathbf{b},$ as expected." #edb/ref 13194139534863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107182 "We can categorize polygons in different ways.\n\nA polygon is **simple** if its boundary does not intersect itself. Otherwise, the polygon is **complex**.\n\n![](images/71/71ef7dbf87cb3a097ec153eb4c4e18920c2567e6a239a788f4340a0a5f33ea6f.png)\n\nA polygon is **convex** if it has no angles pointing inwards. Otherwise, if the polygon has angles pointing inwards, we call it **concave**.\n\n![](images/ef/ef7357c0e21760c3e7f159c253c527e35aac869ce16381132a53356dc53455e2.png)\n\nIt might help to remember that a con**cave** polygon looks a bit like the entrance to a **cave** when viewed from the outside. \n\n![](images/11/11e9844e49a5c586ec573e08865eb7d02dd68459e90df90749116a9eb1575430.png)\n\n**Note**: A more technical way to define a convex polygon is to say that all of its *internal angles* are less than $180^\\circ.$ If you're not sure what an internal angle is yet, don't worry! We'll learn more about the internal angles of a polygon in future lessons." #edb/ref 13194139534885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111806 "A **median** in a triangle is a line segment joining a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $M$ is the midpoint of the side $\\overline{AB}$. Therefore, $\\overline{CM}$ is a median of $\\triangle ABC$.\n\n![](images/42/42504b9e361095557f2861cdaf68d2a092584c43a8ca85b5912e000c2204eeb9.png)\n\nAny triangle has exactly three medians, one for each side. All three medians of $\\triangle ABC$ are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e8/e8ae0de5ee93bf5608573a39eb6003229984d99180007f4f02a2598842ed416b.png)\n\nYou'll notice that the medians intersect at a single point. This is true for *all* triangles. We'll discuss this in more detail shortly." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111807 "We're now ready to state and prove the main result of this lesson.\n\n*Theorem:*\n\n> *All circles are similar.*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n> *Consider a circle $\\mathcal{C}_1$ of radius $R_1$ centered at $A(x_1,y_1)$ and a circle $\\mathcal{C}_2$ of radius $R_2$ centered at $B(x_2,y_2)$. Let $\\mathcal{U}$ be the unit circle centered at the origin.*\n> *First, we will show that*\n> $\\mathcal{C}_1 \\sim \\mathcal{U}$.\n> *For the circle $\\mathcal{C}_1$ of radius $R_1$ centered at $A(x_1,y_1)$, applying the translation $(x,y)\\mapsto (x - x_1, y-y_1)$ followed by a dilation of scale factor $\\dfrac{1}{R_1}$ and center $O$ maps $\\mathcal C_1$ onto $\\mathcal U$.*\n> *Next, we'll show that*\n> $\\mathcal{C}_2 \\sim \\mathcal{U}$.\n> *For the circle $\\mathcal{C}_2$ of radius $R_2$ centered at $B(x_2,y_2)$, applying the translation $(x,y)\\mapsto (x - x_2, y-y_2)$ followed by a dilation of scale factor $\\dfrac{1}{R_2}$ and center $O$ maps $\\mathcal C_2$ onto $\\mathcal U$.*\n> *Now, by the symmetric property of similarity,*\n> $\\mathcal{U} \\sim \\mathcal{C}_2$.\n> *Therefore, by the transitive property of similarity,*\n> $\\mathcal{C}_1 \\sim \\mathcal{C}_2$." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111808 "The centroid has an important property that is often called the **centroid ratio theorem**:\n\n> *The centroid of a triangle splits any median in the proportion $2:1$, starting from the corresponding vertex.*\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $G$ is the centroid, and $\\overline{CK}$ is one of the medians.\n\n![](images/8c/8cc8e92382712aceaa2320dfde9cc38471a8b3c92ea15cdfd1a5280f07e0fb97.png)\n\nAccording to the centroid theorem,\n\n$$\nCG: GK = 2: 1\n$$\n\nThe same proportion holds for the other medians of the triangle. Just remember that the longer partition always contains the vertex.\n\nWe'll prove the centroid ratio theorem in a future lesson." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111809 "The coordinates of a triangle's centroid can be calculated by taking the average of the vertices' $x$- and $y$-coordinates.\n\nNamely, if $A(x_A,y_A)$, $B(x_B,y_B)$, and $C(x_C,y_C)$ are the coordinates of the vertices of a triangle and $G(x_G,y_G)$ is the centroid, then\n\n$$\nx_{G} = \\frac{x_{A} + x_{B} + x_{C}}{3}, y_{G} = \\frac{y_{A} + y_{B} + y_{C}}{3}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this result in a future lesson. But for now, let's see some examples." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111810 "So far, we've proved that a circle $\\mathcal C$ is similar to the unit circle $\\mathcal U$ in cases involving just a single similarity transformation.\n\nBefore we move on to more complex cases, we note that similarity in two-dimensional figures possesses the properties of reflexivity, symmetricity, and transitivity.\n\nMore formally, suppose that $\\mathcal F_1, \\mathcal F_2$, and $\\mathcal F_3$ are two-dimensional geometric figures. Then, the following properties hold:\n\n- The **reflexive** property of similarity:\n$\\mathcal F_1 \\sim \\mathcal F_1$\nThis states that every figure is similar to itself.\n- The **symmetric** property of similarity:\n$\\textrm{If}\\: \\mathcal F_1 \\sim \\mathcal F_2, \\textrm{then}\\: \\mathcal F_2 \\sim \\mathcal F_1$\nThis states that if the first figure is similar to the second, the second must be similar to the first.\n- The **transitive** property of similarity:\n$\\textrm{If}\\: F_1 \\sim \\mathcal F_2 \\:\\textrm{and}\\: F_2 \\sim \\mathcal F_3,\\textrm{then}\\: F_1 \\sim \\mathcal F_3$\nThis states that if the first figure is similar to the second, and the second is similar to the third, the first must be similar to the third.\n\nLet's see how these properties are used to prove that a circle is similar to the unit circle in cases where two similarity transformations are needed." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111811 "![](images/4b/4bd029a874d6e7f3c7a0dd57862ee850be6d5fad4a7bfb57e7ef25800f114df9.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a circle $\\mathcal{C}$ of radius $2$ centered at the origin $O$ and the unit circle $\\mathcal{U}$.\n\n*Claim:*\n\n$$\n\\mathcal C\\sim\\mathcal U\n$$\n\n*Proof:*\n\n> *To prove that $\\mathcal C$ is similar to $\\mathcal U$, we construct a sequence of similarity transformations that map $\\mathcal{C}$ onto $\\mathcal{U}$.*\n> *A dilation of scale factor $\\dfrac12$ and center $O$ maps the circle $\\mathcal{C}$ onto the circle $\\mathcal{U}$.*\n> *Therefore, $\\mathcal{C} \\sim \\mathcal{U}$.*\n\nLet's take a look at another example." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111812 "To prove that *any* two circles $\\mathcal C_1$ and $\\mathcal C_2$ are similar, we can use the following strategy:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find a similarity transformation that maps $\\mathcal C_1$ onto $\\mathcal U$. This shows that $\\mathcal C_1 \\sim \\mathcal U$.\n- **Step 2:** Find a similarity transformation that maps $\\mathcal C_2$ onto $\\mathcal U$. This shows that $\\mathcal C_2 \\sim \\mathcal U$.\n- **Step 3:** Since similarity is *symmetric*, we can write the similarity statement from step 2 as $\\mathcal U \\sim \\mathcal C_2$.\n- **Step 4:** Since $\\mathcal C_1 \\sim \\mathcal U$ and $\\mathcal U \\sim \\mathcal C_2$, by the transitive property of similarity, we have $\\mathcal C_1 \\sim\\mathcal C_2$, as required.\n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111813 "Recall that Prim's algorithm finds a minimum spanning tree of a connected, weighted graph $G$. It follows a so-called greedy approach by starting from an arbitrary vertex and expanding the tree by adding the smallest edge that connects a visited vertex to an unvisited vertex.\n\nOne advantage of Prim's algorithm over other spanning-tree algorithms is that it can easily be applied to a distance matrix. The steps of Prim's algorithm applied to a distance matrix are as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Begin by marking the column corresponding to the starting vertex.\n- **Step 2:** Cancel out the row corresponding to the starting vertex.\n- **Step 3:** While not all columns are marked, find the smallest distance $s$ among all the marked columns. Identify the corresponding row and column, denoted as $u$ and $v$. Then, mark the column $u$ to indicate that the vertex has been added to the tree. Cancel out the row $u$ corresponding to $s$ to prevent revisiting, and add the edge $v-u$ to the tree.\n- **Step 4:** Repeat this process until all vertices are included in the minimum spanning tree, ensuring that the tree grows by selecting the smallest available edge at each step.\n\nThe pseudocode for Prim's algorithm on a distance matrix is the following:\n\n> `Mark the column corresponding to the starting vertex`\n> `Cancel out the row corresponding to the starting vertex`\n> `while (not all columns are marked):`\n> `Find the smallest distance s over all the marked columns`\n> `Let u and v be the row and column corresponding to s`\n> `Mark the column u corresponding to s`\n> `Cancel out the row u corresponding to s`\n> `Add edge v-u to the tree`\n\nIt's easiest to understand the algorithm by following a concrete example, so let's do that." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111814 "Some linear equations have **infinitely many solutions.** \n\nFor example, consider the following equation: \n\n$$\nx+5 = x + 5\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation by subtracting $x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =x+5 \\\\ x+5-x & =x+5-x \\\\ 5 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, what does $x$ equal? The statement $5=5$ is *always* true, irrespective of the value of $x.$ This tells us that *any* value of $x$ is a solution to the equation!\n\nDon't believe it? If we substitute any value, say $x=3,$ into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =x+5 \\\\ 3+5 & =3+5 \\\\ 8 & =8.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's important to note that substituting *any* value for $x$ into the equation will lead to a true statement. For example, the values \n\n$$\nx=42,\\qquad x=-\\dfrac 1 4, \\qquad x=1.2345\n$$\n\nare *all* solutions to the original equation.\n\nTo summarize:\n\n- Our original equation has *infinitely many solutions.*\n\n- The solution of the equation is \"*any (real) value of* $x$\"." #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111815 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2qx = 10x,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. If we're given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{10}_{\\large b} \\ x\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n$$\na = 2q, \\qquad b = 10.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b.$ Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2q = 10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2q & =10 \\\\ \\frac{2q}{2} & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ q & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2qx = 10x$ has infinitely many solutions if $q = 5.$\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by substituting $q = 5$ into our original equation. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2qx & =10x \\\\ 2(5)x & =10x \\\\ 10x & =10x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand simplifying this equation by subtracting $10x$ from both sides yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x & =10x \\\\ 10x-10x & =10x-10x \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is true for all values of $x.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" #edb/ref 13194139535291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111816 "|  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ | $v_5$ |\n| $v_1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $8$ | $26$ | $-$ |\n| $v_2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $13$ | $19$ | $3$ |\n| $v_3$ | $8$ | $13$ | $-$ | $11$ | $-$ |\n| $v_4$ | $26$ | $19$ | $11$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $v_5$ | $-$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nLet's use Prim's algorithm to find the minimum spanning tree of the weighted graph G with the distance matrix given above.\n\nWe start from the vertex $v_1$, and proceed as follows:\n\n| **Step 1.** Mark column $v_1$ by placing a \"$1$\" above it, and cancel out row $v_1$. |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_2$$-$$-$$13$$19$$3$$v_3$$8$$13$$-$$11$$-$$v_4$$26$$19$$11$$-$$-$$v_5$$-$$3$$-$$-$$-$ | Added edgesLength |\n| **Step 2.** The smallest unselected value in the marked column is $8$, located at the intersection of row $v_3$ and column $v_1$. So, we mark column $v_3$ by placing a \"$2$\" above it, cancel out row $v_3$, and add edge $v_1-v_3$ to the tree. |  |\n| $1$$2$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_2$$-$$-$$13$$19$$3$$v_3$$8$$\\color{lightgrey}13$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_4$$26$$19$$11$$-$$-$$v_5$$-$$3$$-$$-$$-$ | Added edgesLength$v_1 - v_3$$8$ |\n| **Step 3.** The smallest unselected value in the marked columns is $11$, located at the intersection of row $v_4$ and column $v_3$. So, we mark column $v_4$, cancel out row $v_4$, and add edge $v_3-v_4$ to the tree. |  |\n| $1$$2$$3$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_2$$-$$-$$13$$19$$3$$v_3$$8$$\\color{lightgrey}13$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_4$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}19$$11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_5$$-$$3$$-$$-$$-$ | Added edgesLength$v_1 - v_3$$8$$v_3 - v_4$$11$ |\n| **Step 4.** The smallest unselected value in the marked columns is $13$, located at the intersection of row $v_2$ and column $v_3$. So, we mark column $v_2$, cancel out row $v_2$, and add edge $v_3-v_2$ to the tree. |  |\n| $1$$4$$2$$3$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_2$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$13$$\\color{lightgrey}19$$\\color{lightgrey}3$$v_3$$8$$\\color{lightgrey}13$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_4$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}19$$11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_5$$-$$3$$-$$-$$-$ | Added edgesLength$v_1 - v_3$$8$$v_3 - v_4$$11$$v_3 - v_2$$13$ |\n| **Step 5.** The smallest unselected value in the marked columns is $3$, located at the intersection of row $v_5$ and column $v_2$. So, we mark column $v_5$, cancel out row $v_5$, and add edge $v_2-v_5$ to the tree. |  |\n| $1$$4$$2$$3$$5$$v_1$$v_2$$v_3$$v_4$$v_5$$v_1$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}8$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_2$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$13$$\\color{lightgrey}19$$\\color{lightgrey}3$$v_3$$8$$\\color{lightgrey}13$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_4$$\\color{lightgrey}26$$\\color{lightgrey}19$$11$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$v_5$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$3$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$$\\color{lightgrey}-$ | Added edgesLength$v_1 - v_3$$8$$v_3 - v_4$$11$$v_3 - v_2$$13$$v_2 - v_5$$3$ |\n\nEvery vertex is now in our tree (every column has been marked), so the process is completed. The list of edges in our minimum spanning tree is given by the table on the right, and the total weight of the resulting minimum spanning tree is\n\n$$\n8 + 11 + 13 + 3 = 35\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111817 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(s - 2)x + t = 6x + 9,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the values of $s$ and $t?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax+b = cx+d{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(s-2)}_{\\large a} \\ x + \\underbrace{t}_{\\large b} = \\underbrace{6}_{\\large c} \\ x + \\underbrace{9}_{\\large d}\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax+b = cx+d$ has infinitely many solutions when $a=c$ and $b=d.$\n\nSo, to determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}})x + {\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}x + {\\color{red}{{9}}}\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we **equate the coefficients** of $x,$ and we **equate the constants:**\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{red}{{9}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $s = 8$ and $t=9,$ our original equation has infinitely many solutions." #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111818 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nFor example, each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x &=2x,\\,\\frac{1}{3}x=\\frac{1}{3}x\\,-2.16x=-2.16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo show that the general equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b,$ we can substitute $a$ for $b$ in our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =bx \\\\ ax & =(a)x \\\\ ax & =ax\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =ax \\\\ ax-ax & =ax-ax \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation, $0=0,$ is always true, regardless of the value of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b,$ and any value of $x$ is a solution to the equation." #edb/ref 13194139536396 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186111819 "Each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n2x + 1 = 2x + 1, \\qquad \\dfrac13x -2 = \\dfrac13x -2, \\qquad 1-x = 1 - x\n$$\n\nMore generally, suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d,\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. Then, this equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nNote that if we substitute $c=a$ and $d=b$ into this equation, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =cx+d \\\\ ax+b & =(a)x+(b) \\\\ ax+b & =ax+b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =ax+b \\\\ ax+b-ax & =ax+b-ax \\\\ b & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation, $b = b,$ is always true. Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions, and any real value of $x$ is a solution." #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111820 "The following data set shows the total time, in hours, that a group of students spent studying for a final exam:\n\n$$\n0, \\quad 1, \\quad 1, \\quad 1, \\quad 2, \\quad 3,\\quad 3, \\quad 3, \\quad 3\n$$\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that we can represent this data using a frequency table.\n\n| Number of Hours Studied | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0$ | $\\color{blue}1$ |\n| $1$ | $\\color{blue}3$ |\n| $2$ | $\\color{blue}1$ |\n| $3$ | $\\color{blue}4$ |\n\nWe interpret the data in this table as follows:\n\n- $\\color{blue}1$ student spent zero hours studying\n- $\\color{blue}3$ students spent one hour studying\n- $\\color{blue}1$ student spent two hours studying\n- $\\color{blue}4$ students spent three hours studying\n\nThis is an example of a **one-way frequency table** because it shows the relationship between a single *variable* (the number of hours studied) and the *frequency* of each value of the variable.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to represent data with two categorical variables." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111821 "![](images/b2/b27906f92121928be444d3b7f616becbcc7a849e1313ef50accd8c8bfd718bfb.png)\n\nIf we know two points on a parabola with *identical* $y$-values, we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking an average of the two $x$-values. \n\nFor example, consider the parabola $y=f(x)$ above. Notice that the values of $f(x)$ at the points $x=-3$ and $x=5$ are equal.\n\n$$\nf(-3) = f(5) = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola's axis of symmetry must be equidistant from the lines $x=-3$ and $x=5.$ We can find this by taking the average of these two values.\n\nIf the parabola's vertex is $(h,k),$ then we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{-3+5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h=1$ is the $x$-coordinate of the vertex, and the axis of symmetry of the parabola is $x=1.$" #edb/ref 13194139535913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111822 "Just as we can have a system of linear equations, we can also have systems of linear inequalities. For example, the system\n\n![](images/42/422ff3fd1ff0704d34d5d2f098e3c524f409d2a4da7d6fa3d792020bbc8ac56d.svg)\nis solved by the points that satisfy the inequalities ![](images/23/23f2bda2195f51e1cbf95f6905d547dc9004733d89866ae9f92ef046c330417c.svg) *and* ![](images/87/877dcbf886336f3054b3df8910cee950868e2819e007cf11544a6be7f15b81d3.svg) simultaneously.\n\nTo find the points that satisfy both inequalities, we first draw the graph of the inequality ![](images/9a/9a758452dbe8e0ede75fb97296110356c18e46275000ac28ebf631d91fa05b22.svg)\n\n![](images/6f/6f3f7b6eb3109225256c5f4342333d03c19ef31b40d5ae5b24c136950a31f76a.png)\n\nNow, on top of the diagram above, we draw the graph of the second inequality, ![](images/c1/c1e37a6573ce7b419aeff619c181b43325304505c05b669394ef0818ace2555f.svg)\n\n![](images/e0/e017eefa235ac4b95009570c59fe5e5587f50bb02299305cfb9126573c0156bd.png)\n\nThe solution to the system is the *intersection* of the two regions (i.e., the part that is shaded twice).\n\n![](images/c1/c1d9b4358e15ca09e8358f8e298fc0ffa745b885adcd7ea522e07f1d221a2c90.png)\n\nThe important thing to realize is that *any* point in the shaded region above satisfies *both* inequalities." #edb/ref 13194139533439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111823 "The **power of quotient rule** states that if we want to raise the quotient $\\dfrac{x}{y}$ to some power $n,$ we must raise the numerator and denominator to the power $n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^n=\\dfrac{x^n}{y^n}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^4=\\dfrac{x^4}{y^4}.\n$$\n\nTo see why the power of quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again the long way. We get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x}{y})}^{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{x}{y} & = \\\\ \\frac{x\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x}{y\\cdot y\\cdot y\\cdot y} & = \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power of quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above." #edb/ref 13194139535314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111824 "Sometimes, we might need to use more than one rule of exponents to simplify an expression. First, let's remind ourselves of all of the rules of exponents.\n\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n\n- The power rule: $\\left( a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nAs an example, let's simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(u^2 v\\right)^3\n$$\n\nWe start by using the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}^{2}v)}^{3}={({u}^{2})}^{3}\\cdot {v}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}^{2})}^{3}\\cdot {v}^{3} & = \\\\ {u}^{(2\\cdot 3)}\\cdot {v}^{3} & = \\\\ {u}^{6}\\cdot {v}^{3} & = \\\\ {u}^{6}{v}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111825 "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $y=2^x.$ First, we create a table of values:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y = 2^x$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/e9/e987a8647092c836bde4bcea31dc075e80e9da36a67133fc2b9431b99c5e42ed.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1\\. The graph has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1).$\n\n2\\. The graph is increasing.\n\n3\\. For $x>0,$ the graph grows very rapidly as $x$ increases. Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow \\infty.$\n\n4\\. For $x<0,$ the graph gets very close to zero as $x$ decreases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has the horizontal asymptote $y=0.$ Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty.$\n\n5\\. The domain is $x\\in (-\\infty , \\infty )$, and the range is $y>0.$\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve $y=a^x$ with $a>1.$ These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/48/48f7bc7bb25bf1955d1e2cf48cffbf09e17f80a1228cada1c31f7ad4822bdace.png)" #edb/ref 13194139535458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111826 "Multiplying an exponential growth function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve ![](images/68/68dcb337cb0927d43403eebeb21c0a54dc46a136f7cd21ce821d9bfc325c748e.svg) represents a vertical scaling of ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) by a factor of ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)  It takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg) This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/70/7052037576cfaf29807f0ad332100b9929a72806846219da2599c8f911500d87.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remained the same, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moved from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/43/437619dfcf8db5be350de504dc9d98eab13c2ac21c62998d3c268a1f475a30d9.svg)\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve ![](images/8c/8ccfcf186ac190ccb8f91676958e6030b4224bfd46b3598261736fbfcb7c351f.svg)\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remains the same, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moves from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/e8/e8c4c8598a8228afb25cbf13c034e2fe234393f9b9c0dfde8be1726b4fc79a8e.svg)" #edb/ref 13194139535458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111827 "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of ![](images/d0/d0a7418e3803197d07b85f03b206b83bed1c4d451397ae953b05634db9a9f829.svg) First, we create a table of values: \n\n| ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) | ![](images/78/78d4465d85b55cee3832a9b598a2564b6a3fa9a072de8a1fb4b40ca884079670.svg) | ![](images/63/63df422d619ca962ca5a69bc7d25dd8831269b92fa5d641dcb960819d3868a9a.svg) | ![](images/66/66ef4b5796888b4025ee2d8b3d516ec80741b341db9389696b6e77a4af5208d8.svg) | ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) | ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) | ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) | ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/e5/e51853c53dfd3d72f8fd380044f7ce864974b3d8c1ca0e690b7caba34fe3456c.svg) | ![](images/c7/c700188055bde6c02303571bfb6b737fddda4a14c08edf1cdeeaf684530eea88.svg) | ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) | ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) | ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) | ![](images/0b/0b47cf9f83219ef6bab2ac82c70ded537ce9db2ab3945b85d5ca678f16b30a75.svg) | ![](images/fc/fce859f2a3c5348530c75d1a07cf8bb4ff0a228bdf8d1e493294b4489b84fdee.svg) | ![](images/8a/8ab6a43799989375f441258e25082fb84a876ad9273de9dba1bc200dca195381.svg) |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/b0/b045833e2e37e2ed4c35e9c8abe8c379dfd7de7eded17d4dc943205677d7fce5.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1\\. The graph has a ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept at ![](images/fd/fd83db97b0675d3527a9f2a7a68d38a98f2d05ab5b1771d961c0d687e44c191b.svg)\n\n2\\. The graph is decreasing.\n\n3\\. For ![](images/e4/e45cbcbc2e3f466cab4e379776bce2640286bc2a918ff0177c0433b97f7cd4b7.svg), the graph grows very rapidly as ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) decreases. Symbolically, we can write ![](images/f5/f54328d6cf80c554acae5f7a9693b0eb4ea745ec0a4b0f5013b03a3e41ae27db.svg) as ![](images/72/7248bd0778a7746f6b1018c7bc926d8e678ba829b741beed092d802bd7ba1233.svg)\n\n4\\. For ![](images/ee/ee98d74fc058e95e72d13a5e575e70e4d5adedd26413e3f01ab1e3f788be87e8.svg), the graph gets very close to zero as ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) increases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has a horizontal asymptote ![](images/6c/6c63827e94b6c11a9803605bd54156d90d3fcc6ff4639dd179a5fb024bcffaa1.svg) Symbolically, we can write ![](images/43/43a6d025efce6b330ba7907d1daa54ad58cf04feee07adedad66d4c806b5f88d.svg) as ![](images/f9/f9b318487299434d45096742eaad8ea148abb88c27b718c1bc0983f927dc9911.svg)\n\n5\\. The domain is ![](images/04/043681a7084203dcb8609f78989cadb8f0bcd701978361940d93ef850da95a14.svg), and the range is ![](images/95/95d0bc38cd8737723521d2a1846cb71a0dba33d3df26bd73c18acbb650fc8b95.svg)\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve ![](images/90/90517bb7bc12cfec9d00d88592bbda7572f4a0c5cbcf4f66049311d42f20f6ca.svg) with ![](images/e2/e276af13a21df7f4755de7b18da1a10ca590bbdf3387515efa0896f6cddb79cf.svg) These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/0f/0f8e7cc0db94f5f51bc83404351a7708171fdce50e2946b51263885e4230ecef.png)" #edb/ref 13194139535458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111828 "Multiplying an exponential decay function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve ![](images/d5/d59881254ee1a66445dcf2697e8af4426cbe9d219443d5f40ef2aefc426d598b.svg) represents a vertical scaling of ![](images/fb/fb1255ff2ad78ef15b03968cb806543d4f29f1029680068f76c913bb4cab96ad.svg) by a factor of ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)\nIt takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/fb/fb1255ff2ad78ef15b03968cb806543d4f29f1029680068f76c913bb4cab96ad.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg) This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/d5/d5cf96822f65e51d618b651174f1c516eb97be3bf61f5b1380d1c245d29a5a72.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remained the same, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moved from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/43/437619dfcf8db5be350de504dc9d98eab13c2ac21c62998d3c268a1f475a30d9.svg)\n\nOn the other hand, the curve\n![](images/99/99f750a011acdbe86e9c9397d5e5901c686a2c10b625fa9dc98a0b90a516c592.svg)\nrepresents a vertical scaling of\n![](images/c8/c8c077b168d3c60c74b354ee942893abefb51b5e08b7483044ea5ea2edf50795.svg)\nby a factor of ![](images/31/31659384627c9aa2540de6fca01634acdcdd4c77dd705fbf922c813cc23e822f.svg) It takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/c8/c8c077b168d3c60c74b354ee942893abefb51b5e08b7483044ea5ea2edf50795.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/31/31659384627c9aa2540de6fca01634acdcdd4c77dd705fbf922c813cc23e822f.svg) This has the effect of **compressing** the curve downwards.\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched or compressed exponential curve ![](images/8c/8ccfcf186ac190ccb8f91676958e6030b4224bfd46b3598261736fbfcb7c351f.svg)\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remains the same, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moves from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/e8/e8c4c8598a8228afb25cbf13c034e2fe234393f9b9c0dfde8be1726b4fc79a8e.svg)" #edb/ref 13194139535458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111829 "The **zero product rule** says that if we have the equation\n\n$$\na\\cdot b = 0\n$$\n\nthen $a=0$ or $b=0$.\n\nIn other words, if we know that the product of two numbers is zero, then at least one of them must be zero because any number multiplied by zero is zero.\n\nThe zero product rule is useful for solving quadratic equations. For example, let's take a look at the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(x-1)}{\\color{blue}(x+2)} = 0\n$$\n\nWhenever a quadratic equation is factored on the left-hand side and has zero on the right-hand side, we can apply the zero product rule to solve the equation. So for our equation, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}x-1=0} \\qquad\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\qquad {\\color{blue}x+2 =0}\n$$\n\nWe solve each of the equations separately, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x - 1 &= 0 & x + 2|= 0 \\\\ x|= 1 & x|=-2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x = -2,1$.\n\nAs usual, we can check our solutions by substituting them back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x &= -2 & x|= 1 \\\\ ⇓ & ⇓ \\\\ (x - 1)(x + 2)|= 0 & (x - 1)(x + 2)|= 0 \\\\ (-2 - 1)(-2 + 2)|= 0 & (1 - 1)(1 + 2)|= 0 \\\\ (-3)(-0)|= 0 & (0)(3)|= 0 \\\\ 0|= 0✓ & 0|= 0✓\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111830 "Suppose that we want to solve the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n2x^2-3x+1=0\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to factor the trinomial on the left-hand side. We'll need to find two numbers such that\n\n- their product is equal to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $2 \\times 1 = 2$), and\n- their sum is equal to the linear coefficient (which is $-3$).\n\nThese two numbers are $-1$ and $-2$. So, we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 3x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2}- 1x - 2x_(⏟)_(-3x) + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(2x^{2} - x) + (-2x + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx(2x - 1) - 1(2x - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)(2x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nx-1=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad 2x-1=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=1 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{2},1\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111831 "Remember that for a quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nthe discriminant\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = b^2 - 4ac\n$$\n\ntells us a lot about the number of solutions:\n\n- if $\\mathcal{D}>0$, then we get $2$ distinct real solutions,\n- if $\\mathcal{D}=0$, then we get $1$ distinct real solution, and\n- if $\\mathcal{D}<0$, then we get no real solutions.\n\nIn the same way, the number of roots of a quadratic function\n\n$$\ny=ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\ntells us a lot about the discriminant:\n\n- if the graph has $2$ roots, then $\\mathcal{D}>0$,\n- if the graph has $1$ root, then $\\mathcal{D}=0$, and\n- if the graph has no roots, then $\\mathcal{D}<0$.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the graph of the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny=-x^2-x+1\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/93/9348e9472f8080c572caf81c07da53892d5398ca69e6263595e27d85e56bc803.png)\n\nIn the graph, the parabola intersects the $x$-axis twice, so it has $2$ roots. Therefore, we must have $\\mathcal{D}>0$. Indeed, if we calculate $\\mathcal D$ explicitly, we get a positive result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (-1)^{2} - 4 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 1 - (-4) \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general relation between the discriminant $\\mathcal{D}$ of a quadratic function\n\n$$\ny= ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nand the function's graph is sketched below. Remember that the leading coefficient $a$ determines which way the parabola opens: upward if $a>0$, and downward if $a<0$.\n\n![](images/22/22f69c3bd70a732e7717d801a2e2d79eae685b6cd1ceb8c5c45788339859fc7b.png)" #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111832 "Suppose we toss a ball vertically upwards with initial velocity\n\n$$\nu=24.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}\n$$\n\nas shown below. How do we determine the ball's height after $1$ second?\n\n![](images/95/95a4afeb9f5c40894d10cff245518db69b5508ccbf82789f1e3878658a5c47c5.png)\n\nThe displacement of an object after being projected vertically upward can be modeled using the equation\n\n$$\ns=ut + \\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nLet's recall what each variable represents:\n\n- $s$ is the displacement from the starting position, measured in meters.\n- $u$ is the initial velocity, measured in meters per second.\n- $t$ is time, measured in seconds.\n- $g=-9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\n**Important**: In this situation, we take the constant $g$ to be *negative* because the ball moves *against* gravity. In other words, the ball travels upward, yet gravity is trying to pull the ball back down to Earth.\n\nTo determine the height of the ball after one second, we substitute\n\n$$\nu = 24.5, t=1\n$$\n\nand $g=-9.8$ into our equation for $s$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= ut + \\frac{1}{2}gt^{2} \\\\\n&= (24.5)(1) + \\frac{1}{2}(-9.8)(1)^{2} \\\\\n&= 24.5 - 4.9 \\\\\n&= 19.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after one second, the ball is $19.6\\,\\textrm{m}$ above the ground." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111833 "The curve ![](images/e9/e910a01694746b83e1662e73779d7e758399e2d0bdf7441bbd8221a37f278b17.svg) represents a vertical scaling of ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) by a factor of ![](images/79/79cadb0dadf367d4c7456e91f5847fa57d3f90d5129f383ca6c073782ecc6892.svg) It takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/79/79cadb0dadf367d4c7456e91f5847fa57d3f90d5129f383ca6c073782ecc6892.svg) This has the effect of squashing, or **compressing**, the curve downwards.\n\n![](images/68/685e360ed7bcd7975aa705d192ef487af17688bb113b20e10f881a0bdf7f46b7.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remained the same, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moved from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/97/97a51f546ed69cb656e8a811f3a4acf3f6dacce8e9ed7cac63419ad63f0497d1.svg)\n\nIn general for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve ![](images/8c/8ccfcf186ac190ccb8f91676958e6030b4224bfd46b3598261736fbfcb7c351f.svg)\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remains the same, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moves from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/e8/e8c4c8598a8228afb25cbf13c034e2fe234393f9b9c0dfde8be1726b4fc79a8e.svg)" #edb/ref 13194139535458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111834 "When two line segments have the same length, they are **congruent** segments. To indicate that the line segments $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{CD}$ are congruent we write \n\n$$\n\\overline{AB} \\:\\:{\\color{red}\\boldsymbol{\\cong}}\\:\\: \\overline{CD},\n$$\n\nand we use special marks like in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d7fd26fce7dce67fa57769db3e96be503051be5c7d2c7714ed48927547d5165.png)\n\nWe can also write \n\n$$\nAB \\:{\\color{blue}=}\\: CD,\n$$\n\nwhich means that the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ is equal to the measure of $\\overline{CD}.$\n\n**Watch out!** The equation \n\n$$\n\\overline{AB} = \\overline{CD}\n$$\n\nis only true when both $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{CD}$ correspond to the same exact segment. When the segments are distinct, we should use the symbol $\\cong$ to represent that the two segments are separate but that they have the same length." #edb/ref 13194139534977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111835 "The **midpoint** of a line segment is the point that splits it into two segments of equal length. For example, $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ede582389ad936b02a6074a3fb6543e730ae955baf9784147f133f9492a35708.png)\n\nThe midpoint splits the line segment into two congruent segments,\n\n$$\n\\overline{PM}\\cong \\overline{QM}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is twice as long as the segments $\\overline{PM}$ and $\\overline{QM}$:\n\n$$\nPQ = 2PM, \\qquad PQ = 2QM\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111836 "When two angles share a common side, they are called **adjacent** angles. When two angles are adjacent, we can add their measures to find the measure of the larger angle. \n\nFor example, consider the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f5/f562f3505de0dd2345b29e2baabaf51a7885cbccb5d95e6201b8bc9aec1b1986.png)\n\nThe angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent because they both have the side $\\overrightarrow{SR}$ in common.\n\nSince the angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent, we can find the measure of the combined angle $\\angle PST$ by adding the two angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠PST & ={{m∠PSR}_{⏟}}_{\\text{orange angle}}+{{m∠RST}_{⏟}}_{\\text{blue angle}} \\\\  & ={95}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={140}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139534679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111837 "Some angles have special names.\n\n- A **right angle** has a measure of $90^\\circ.$ It corresponds to one-quarter of a full turn.\n\n![](images/15/155a1d6d2abd868b05674dec47ef4b20a5147d30cd328a5ba53d00dea271d65c.png)\n\n- A **straight angle**, which is just a straight line, has a measure of $180^\\circ.$ It corresponds to one-half of a full turn.\n\n![](images/24/2423950f80de8354aee74c4f1daa6e42cf376caa0365307c76ebc7e09f96c8cf.png)\n\n- A **null angle** has a measure of $0^\\circ.$ You can imagine \"closing\" the angle until the two sides of the angle overlap. In this case, we don't have any turn, so its measure is zero!\n\n![](images/f8/f86b7c1b7ba758fb6b07b6aeabd03395d29103b45e4cd220444b675f70a6e630.png)\n\n- A **full angle** has a measure of $360^\\circ.$ You can imagine \"opening\" the angle until one of the sides goes all the way around. So, this is the angle between a ray and itself after it has made one full turn.\n\n![](images/cb/cbc9e288f4d3905357539a221954328ef0ac799531fab62f034364d32a7c0f29.png)\n\nOn a diagram, null and full angles can look the same, so it is up to us what we choose to call it." #edb/ref 13194139534657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111838 "When two lines meet at a point, they create two pairs of opposite angles. The angles in each pair are called **vertical angles**.\n\nVertical angles are *always* congruent.\n\n![](images/0d/0d5b2895ae18beb7242ef666af08e5b8e3490880de99aec8d993b02f31a9fcf4.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $\\angle 1$ and $\\angle 3$ are vertical angles and are therefore congruent. \n\nLikewise, $\\angle 2$ and $\\angle 4$ are vertical angles and are therefore congruent. \n\nSo, we have $\\angle 1 \\cong \\angle 3$ and $\\angle 2 \\cong \\angle 4.$" #edb/ref 13194139535300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111839 "We categorize angles that are not right, straight, or full into three groups.\n\n- An **acute angle** has a measure between $0^\\circ$ and $90^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ec/ec35031af65d0ea9e08121d3df02ed136c5b81b2f90698f4a51681657e67de48.png)\n\n- An **obtuse angle** has a measure between $90^\\circ$ and  $180^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/a9/a96bdfed918f56e7f5cc1cfe9eaad42dcd32a54e663c78fead08e68e6ef80865.png)\n\n- Angles whose measure is between $180^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ$ are called **reflex angles**.  \n\n![](images/62/62323a85f8b3357628c2d221f0293c94ec4c3d9e6e8d1ca65400d2ea7349d3ce.png)" #edb/ref 13194139534939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111840 "When solving problems with angles, it is helpful to remember the following classifications:\n\n- a **null** angle has a measure of $0^\\circ,$\n\n- an **acute** angle has a measure between $0^\\circ$ and $90^\\circ,$\n\n- a **right** angle has a measure of $90^\\circ,$\n\n- an **obtuse** angle has a measure between $90^\\circ$ and $180^\\circ,$\n\n- a **straight** angle has a measure of $180^\\circ,$\n\n- a **reflex** angle has a measure between $180^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ,$ and\n\n- a **full** angle has a measure of $360^\\circ.$\n\nIt is also helpful to remember that when two lines meet at a point, the opposite angles are called **vertical angles**. Vertical angles are congruent, and consequently they have the same measure." #edb/ref 13194139535314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111841 "We know that when a transversal crosses two parallel lines, a set of $8$ interesting angles are formed, some of which are congruent and others of which are supplementary.\n\n![](images/c9/c97f50b95a07ee2a0c4e45056cf70dff89daad38d5b6e3465b8b08738e2b4be9.png)\n\nThe reverse is also true: when certain angles formed by a transversal are congruent or supplementary, then the two lines that the transversal crosses must be parallel.\n\nMore precisely, two lines are parallel if they are crossed by a transversal such that:\n\n- the **corresponding angles are congruent**:\n\n![](images/40/401774d798eeb40a2d73afab6f03c3863fd55dd9c9cd694bf622f8fc9cc72f2b.png)\n\n- or, the **alternate (interior or exterior) angles are congruent**\n\n![](images/c5/c52363f3967c61f20b148bbca9f545b2d95332b470fc3afac15d58bd2571b38a.png)\n\n- or, the **consecutive (interior or exterior) angles are supplementary**.\n\n![](images/ca/ca6561632b9a08c9ea87f2d51786ef3539dac40d089621631f5ce8f7b0556cd6.png)\n\n![](images/13/1309b5bea4f93b87c2747ebd3a1b721fbaa5a6df2999c1a06936164574936ef4.png)\n\nIf at least one pair of angles satisfies the conditions above, then the two lines are parallel.\n\n**Watch out:** When two lines are parallel, we also know that the vertical angles are congruent and the adjacent are supplementary. These, however, are true conditions for any pair of crossing lines, and therefore cannot be used as criteria for parallel lines." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111842 "An angle **bisector** is a line that divides an angle into two equal parts. \n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AC}$ is the bisector of the angle $\\angle BAD.$\n\n![](images/3b/3bf65e915f4fc26b8d87199f46ea0bc514195d857890983d31aa2d73eb38a811.png)\n\nThe resulting angles $\\angle BAC$ and $\\angle CAD$ are congruent (they have the same measure)." #edb/ref 13194139535458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111843 "Consider the following triangle, where the side ![](images/72/721aa9559f1b9f3da3e69b1b9763327e29a6f3fb77157f4b42dd076d3865e098.svg) has been extended beyond the vertex ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg)\n\n![](images/6b/6be676cc487d17815701f3783e9eca71d7491a0e63d7ae9016fb65ad801e61d6.png)\n\n  \n\nThe resulting angle ![](images/f1/f1729f131b4ec04b45c992a99498753dd09d27e76e917dff66d1fd554ec2ab5e.svg) is called the **exterior angle** at vertex ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg) \n\nExterior angles have the following properties:\n\n- An exterior angle and its corresponding interior angle form a linear pair and are therefore supplementary. Here, we have\n![](images/9a/9ae2a4aa323b941eff72a50f5c1086c72e961d0eed919a327306e77cfd5a7ef6.svg)\n\n- The measure of an exterior angle is equal to the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent interior angles. This is called the **exterior angles theorem**. To see why it is true, notice that since the internal angles of a triangle sum to ![](images/1a/1ace39b6f52fbf0b8654fc88bfa3e97366d33e3c411580da106e513a498aca53.svg) we have \n![](images/49/49aafbdb975ccd101e16c921c20630af6296b860438ebfb62d8a205cc6e7b3c7.svg)\n\n- At each vertex of a triangle, there are two exterior angles. They are vertical angles, and consequently, they have the same measure.\n\n![](images/b4/b459ad1edd5e0fdf512d722eb55ab20ff66c281d7160077d12936a6926529aa0.png)" #edb/ref 13194139535455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111844 "Yet another important fact about exterior angles is:\n\nThe sum of the measures of the exterior angles of a triangle is always equal to $360^\\circ.$\n\nSo, for the triangle below, we have \n\n$$\nx+y+z = 360^\\circ.\n$$\n\n![](images/22/22586791e1118a38a554deffaec3ac5b73eafc47eee4be2052c0949dde584cea.png)\n\nTo see why, first recall that the measure of an exterior angle is equal to the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent interior angles. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =m∠B+m∠C \\\\ y & =m∠A+m∠C \\\\ z & =m∠A+m∠B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that the internal angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =(m∠B+m∠C)+(m∠A+m∠C)+(m∠A+m∠B) \\\\  & =2m∠A+2m∠B+2m∠C \\\\  & =2(m∠A+m∠B+m∠C) \\\\  & =2({180}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 "There are lots of different types of quadrilaterals. Let's look at some examples.\n\n- A **trapezoid** is a quadrilateral with exactly ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) pair of opposite sides that are parallel to each other. \nThe parallel sides are called the **bases**. The other two sides are called the **legs**.\n\n![](images/9f/9fa09c46a2119d35c796065a696954931e20062fd5b3677a5484bef92bc2b0d8.png)\n\n- An **isosceles trapezoid** is a  trapezoid with congruent legs.\n\n![](images/b6/b6b2b2012c1d724f9da8b23551ec4f978f190d86875d7db7a3074635d7f40c02.png)\n\n- A **parallelogram** is a quadrilateral with ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of parallel sides.\n\n \n\n![](images/5d/5d0f383f3510f54b415a7ab99ef52fb30270924f97b8afec59534a96cd3edcef.png)\n\n Opposite sides and opposite angles of a parallelogram are always congruent. Moreover, if a quadrilateral has ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of opposite congruent sides or  ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of opposite congruent angles, it is always a parallelogram.\n\n![](images/39/39ccf26333785f9ef47eed68a96bc40005a6ba025fdb6e250beefe14f10b043d.png)\n\n- A **rectangle** is a parallelogram where all angles are right.\n\n \n\n![](images/55/55a16e0ba93bf1f01d576cca558805aaea9631d1fa8e43b149509d4f8eb4c834.png)\n\n A rectangle where all sides are congruent is a **square**.\n\n![](images/a0/a04b2a0e86be8b085c5e3fc906d5aa66dfea45c033d52cd4746031202a7d427d.png)\n\n- A **rhombus** is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. The opposite angles of a rhombus are also congruent.\n\n![](images/d3/d3588d7cbe8f285198df3909df3c03fdec04f53a3f75769e46a0ab4283fa27b3.png)\n\n A rhombus where all angles are congruent is a **square**.\n\n \n\n![](images/94/941872050d5407bcd9cb532e318276adccff802230ed854a2d292666fc0096fc.png)\n\n- A **kite** is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other.\n\n![](images/11/116e323d81ee190c0f69b4d7cdcefe9f0952da883b0d6d3c6d4036119794baa7.png)" #edb/ref 13194139534982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111846 "Let's consider the trapezoid $ABCD$, shown below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0288582ac48824cc6297b24a7a20680f12f2a27533dd5fc65f03f849c0f1915.png)\n\nNotice that the angles $\\angle A$ and $\\angle D$ are on the same leg. Two angles of a trapezoid that are on the same leg are called **adjacent angles.** Adjacent angles of a trapezoid are always supplementary.\n\nNow, since $\\angle A$ and $\\angle D$ are supplementary, we can solve for $\\angle D$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠D &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n65^{∘} + m∠D &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠D &= 115^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo understand *why* $\\angle A$ and $\\angle D$ are supplementary, notice that we have two parallel lines $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{CD}$ intersected by the transversal $\\overline{AD}$. This means that the angles $\\angle{A}$ and $\\angle{D}$ are consecutive interior angles, and we know that when a transversal intersects two parallel lines, the consecutive interior angles are supplementary.\n\nFinally, notice that we can make the same conclusion about $\\angle B$ and $\\angle C$. So in this trapezoid, we have two pairs of supplementary angles:\n\n$$\nm∠A + m∠D = 180^{∘}, m∠B + m∠C = 180^{∘}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111847 "The length of the largest base of a trapezoid is the sum of the smaller base and the projections of the legs onto the largest base.\n\n![](images/91/9112c92f31b6d6220153c8b65b2524afc7c84ed5a8f41e325ded2315bb9ceb29.png)\n\nFrom the diagram above, we note the following:\n\n- The smaller base is $\\overline{CD}$.\n- The projection of the leg $\\overline{AD}$ onto the largest base is $\\overline{AH}$.\n- The projection of the leg $\\overline{BC}$ onto the largest base is $\\overline{BF}$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nAB = AH + CD + BF\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111848 "A **rectangle** is a parallelogram where all angles are right. It has all the properties of parallelograms: its opposite sides are parallel and congruent.\n\n![](images/1f/1fa96b5993a48483798ff4ac05d75f1ffa271f82e5ed6e61a452eb97b3daf3e4.png)\n\nIf all $4$ sides of a rectangle are congruent, then it is a **square**.\n\n![](images/82/822788813f0e81eebef50f324e1bcaf329e7fa5552e10557a19d20b4e868063e.png)" #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111849 "In geometry, a **translation** means moving an object to the left, right, upwards, or downwards without rotating, resizing, or anything else.\n\nLet's take a look at the two triangles $\\mathcal T$ and $\\mathcal T'$ shown below.\n\n![](images/1a/1ac6f34b6f24cdc44cc796ef2f6a6ee33db96550a95de549ce05046f6f1228e8.png)\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- The original shape, $\\mathcal T$, is called the **preimage**. The shape $\\mathcal T'$, the result of the translation, is called the **image**. In our case, the preimage $\\mathcal T$ has been translated $4$ units to the right and $2$ units upward.\n- It's important to realize that *every point* on $\\mathcal T$ is translated to a point on $\\mathcal T'$. The diagram shows the triangle's vertices being translated, but the same translation applies to every point on each side of the triangle as well.\n- A translation is a type of **transformation**. There are other types of transformations that we'll learn more about later." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111850 "A **reflection** can be thought of as \"mirroring\" or \"flipping\" an object over a **line of reflection.**\n\nWhen a point is reflected in the $x$-axis, the $x$-coordinate remains the same, but the $y$-coordinate is transformed into its opposite (i.e., its sign is changed). The following function can represent this transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (x,-y)\n$$\n\nFor example, reflecting the point $P(3,2)$ in the $x$-axis gives the point $Q(3,-2)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/7f/7fa6735e88a237c48f9bd5d414c66d81b7e204ed106bdea49bc7a315a5b43766.png)\n\nOn the other hand, when a point is reflected in the $y$-axis, the $y$-coordinate remains the same, but the $x$-coordinate is transformed into its opposite (its sign is changed). The following function can represent this transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (-x,y)\n$$\n\nFor example, reflecting the point $P(3,2)$ in the $y$-axis gives the point $R(-3,2)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3eeaf93b2020e5d4b46cf6dccece7128f6fd37ec355dbd63229285aa9126c6b4.png)" #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111851 "Two polygons are **congruent** if they have the same shape. This means that\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nConsider the two polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ below. Are they congruent?\n\n![](images/b0/b0a11815bf9006ccdca0ebe71ff7ba53ac7526c71a715ece71914b103b4a24a8.png)\n\nLet's check these criteria for our polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}.$ We can see that both polygons have $3$ sides, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{AB} & ≅\\overset{―}{XY}\\,, & \\overset{―}{AC} & ≅\\overset{―}{XZ}\\,, & \\overset{―}{BC} & ≅\\overset{―}{YZ}\\,, \\\\ ∠X & ≅∠A\\,, & ∠Y & ≅∠B\\,, & ∠Z & ≅∠C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygons are congruent, and we can write \n\n$$\nABC \\cong XYZ.\n$$\n\nThe order in which we write the symbols is important: the corresponding vertices have to be in the same order.\n\nWe can also write $\\mathcal{P} \\cong \\mathcal{Q}.$" #edb/ref 13194139535343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111852 "The **side-angle-side (SAS) congruence criterion** can be used as a shortcut to prove that two triangles are congruent. It states the following.\n\n> Two triangles are congruent if and only if **two sides and the included angle** of one triangle are congruent to two sides and the included angle of the other triangle.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the triangles below.\n\n![](images/0c/0c1b8e0487771a05854724aab6ab0b6dcbc0fbe7ecabc32bcf0fa72e51ae172b.png)\n\nWe can see that two sides of $\\triangle ABC$ are congruent to two sides of $\\triangle KLM$:\n\n$\\overline{AB}\\cong \\overline{KL}, \\quad \\overline{BC}\\cong \\overline{LM}$.\nAlso, we see that the included angles are congruent:\n$\\angle{B}\\cong \\angle{L}$.\n\nTherefore, the SAS congruence criterion guarantees that these two triangles are congruent, and we can write\n\n$$\n\\triangle ABC \\cong \\triangle KLM\n$$\n\n**Caution:** When using the SAS criterion, it's vital to make sure that the angles you compare are the *included* angles between the two pairs of congruent sides. The SAS criterion only works if the included angles are congruent." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111853 "Recall that the sides of a right triangle have special names.\n\n- The longest side of a right triangle is called the hypotenuse. In the triangle below, the hypotenuse is $\\overline{AB}$.\n- The two other sides are called the legs. In the triangle below, the legs are $\\overline{AC}$ and $\\overline{BC}$.\n\n![](images/9c/9c53fed46a71df4e1271b81230a8c11e09d839fb627a3af0c20abfea612eee4b.png)\n\nFor right triangles, it is straightforward to prove congruence. The **Hypotenuse-Leg (HL)** criterion states the following:\n\n> Two right triangles are congruent if and only if they have congruent hypotenuses and a pair of congruent legs.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the right triangles below.\n\n![](images/38/382dde5533a7b8f1cf35d462af7d0286c960493b07b93b54dae5a1e9b2664116.png)\n\nAs we can see, the right triangles have congruent hypotenuses,\n\n$$\n\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{KM}\n$$\n\nand a pair of congruent legs,\n\n$$\n\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{KL}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the HL congruence criterion guarantees that these two triangles are congruent:\n\n$$\n\\triangle ABC \\cong \\triangle KLM\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111854 "A **composite polygon** is a polygon that is made up of other polygons, such as triangles, parallelograms, rectangles, squares, and others.\n\nFor example, the polygon below is composite. How can we find its area?\n\n![](images/b6/b695538eb3d7d931191f7bd678bf9313aff7db0741ce23606449c81b978ffdc1.png)\n\nFirst, notice that the polygon consists of four squares and one rectangle in the middle.\n\n![](images/f9/f986a728f6f49367ab20d0b00d55a66044c54a6953b170690919a088d3e400bf.png)\n\nTo calculate the area of the composite polygon above, we add the areas of the four squares and the rectangle:\n\n$$\nA = 2A_{1} + 2A_{2} + A_{3}\n$$\n\nThe areas of the squares and the rectangle are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{1} &= 3^{2} = 9, \\\\\nA_{2} &= 2^{2} = 4, \\\\\nA_{3} &= 3 \\cdot 2 = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the area of the entire figure is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= 2 \\cdot 9 + 2 \\cdot 4 + 6 \\\\\n&= 18 + 8 + 6 \\\\\n&= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111855 "In the picture below, we have a rectangle with a smaller rectangular hole. How can we find the area of the shaded region?\n\n![](images/91/9152f36db482504e76edb386be9bd36bccc10bcbdf5a8b09df2cb34dc2c51013.png)\n\nFirst, we must work out the areas of the outer and inner rectangles. Then, we subtract the area of the inner rectangle from the area of the outer rectangle.\n\nThe outer rectangle has sides of length $7\\,\\textrm{cm}$ and $5\\,\\textrm{cm}$. So, its area is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{O} = 7 \\cdot 5 =35\\,\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\nThe inner rectangle has sides of length $4\\,\\textrm{in}$ and $3\\,\\textrm{in}$. So, its area is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{I} = 4 \\cdot 3 =12\\,\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the shaded region is the difference between the areas of the outer and inner rectangles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= A_{O} - A_{I} \\\\\n&= 35 - 12 \\\\\n&= 23cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111856 "Consider the transformation that translates the point $Q(1,2)$ by $4$ units to the right and $2$ units up, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9eae75a222d1cd9eb29b091772d25326fd0957ee4b894b5322a7faaa25048f59.png)\n\nWe can express this transformation by writing it as a function, as follows:\n\n$$\nT(x,y) = (x+4,y+2)\n$$\n\nThe function above takes a point as its input and gives another point as output. Let's check it with our point $Q(1,2){:}$\n\n$$\nT(1, 2) = (1 + 4, 2 + 2) = (5, 4)✓\n$$\n\nAny translation of $a$ units to the right and $b$ units up can be written using any of the following notations:\n\n$$\n(x, y) ↦ (x + a, y + b); T(x, y) = (x + a, y + b)\n$$\n\nIf $a$ is negative, then the function translates the point $(x,y)$ by $\\mid a \\mid$ units to the left. If $b$ is negative, then the function translates the point $(x,y)$ by $\\mid b \\mid$ units downwards." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111857 "Let's find the midpoint $M$ of the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ on a number line, if $A$ corresponds to $x_1= 3$ and $B$ corresponds to $x_2=7,$ as depicted below.\n\n![](images/11/115797e11eb3e434d5faefe412a8cefea216b6190436d408d199ac31d0da7210.png)\n\n \n\nThe midpoint of a line segment splits it into two segments of equal length.\nTo find the number $x_m$ corresponding to $M,$ we use the following **midpoint formula**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{m} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x_1=3$ and $x_2=7$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{m} & =\\frac{3+7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=5.$\n\n![](images/fc/fc0a47734756e12ec117db92d8f0273ae564c03ead88ce86eac82005be6feaf4.png)" #edb/ref 13194139535300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111858 "The diagonals of a rectangle are equal and bisect each other. Consequently, they divide a rectangle into four isosceles triangles, as shown below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f0aafdf04bb7f967122fc8f90454b1b09787e375857bac3bcf5fdf80925e32c.png)\n\nIn a square, the diagonals also bisect the angles.\n\n![](images/cd/cd61fabe993fa6cde5ea4020a659e86b3543597d70a304b3e5ada3abfa426186.png)" #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111859 "Suppose we have a rectangle with vertices $A(0,0)$, $B(3,0)$, $C(3,2)$, and $D(0,2)$, as shown below. How can we calculate its area?\n\n![](images/3e/3e10ebebd7f317a5000d5cbdb96864c1d3fcf5161c537fa015db7ce42b1959fe.png)\n\nIf we find the lengths of the sides of the rectangle, then the area $\\mathcal A$ of a rectangle is simply the product of the sides.\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = AB\\cdot BC\n$$\n\nSince $\\overline{AB}$ is horizontal, the length of this segment is simply the absolute value of the difference between the $x$-coordinates of the endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB = \\begin{vmatrix}x_B - x_A & =\\end{vmatrix}3-0 &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, since $\\overline{AD}$ is vertical, the length of this segment is simply the absolute value of the difference between the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints:\n\n$$\nAD = \\begin{vmatrix}y_D - y_A & = & 2-0\\end{vmatrix} = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of our rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = 3\\cdot 2 = 6\n$$\n\nLet's now take a look at a similar example with a square." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111860 "Let's recall the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}x+{sin}^{2}x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we divide both sides of this identity by ${\\color{black}\\cos^2 x},$ we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cos^2 x} {\\color{black}\\cos^2 x} + \\dfrac {\\sin^2 x}{\\color{black}\\cos^2 x}\t = \\dfrac 1 {\\color{black}\\cos^2 x}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n1+ \\left(\\dfrac {\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\dfrac 1 {\\cos x}\\right)^2 \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nNow, recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}, \\qquad \\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above identities into $(\\ast)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x})}^{2} \\\\ 1+(\\operatorname{tan}x)^{2} & =(\\operatorname{sec}x)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n1 + \\tan^2 x  = \\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nThe final identity is called the **secant-tangent identity**. We often use it to simplify trigonometric expressions containing both tangent and secant functions." #edb/ref 13194139533439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111861 "We can use our knowledge of calculating distances in the coordinate plane to find the perimeter of polygons.\n\nFor example, consider $\\triangle ABC$ below with vertices $A(6,4)$, $B(3,4)$, $C(3,8)$.\n\n![](images/be/befdb725ae46113070852f526e60589b50b6333e8388a88ae5679dc0bbcbe9ed.png)\n\nSince $\\overline{AB}$ is horizontal, we can calculate the length of this segment by computing the absolute value of the difference between the $x$-coordinates of the endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\mid x_{B} - x_{A} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid 6 - 3 \\mid \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, since $\\overline{BC}$ is vertical, we can calculate the length of this segment by computing the absolute value of the difference between the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= \\mid y_{C} - y_{B} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid 8 - 4 \\mid \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the length of $\\overline{AC}$, we use the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= \\sqrt{(x_{C} - x_{A})^{2} + (y_{C} - y_{A})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(3 - 6)^{2} + (8 - 4)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 16} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= AB + AC + BC \\\\\n&= 3 + 5 + 4 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111862 "Any two circles are similar because any circle can be mapped to any other circle using a combination of similarity transformations.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the circles $\\mathcal C$ and $\\mathcal C'$ centered at $O$ and with radii $2$ and $4$, respectively.\n\n![](images/2e/2e4bcff4d12082a68626853544ad9bbef30e465d2c65672e32bbf81d1bb82511.png)\n\nWe can double the radius of $\\mathcal{C}$ so that it has the same radius as $\\mathcal{C}'$. To do this, we use a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $2$.\n\n![](images/5a/5ac24a8a3c795ebb8f197083b07a9a4e5c0a4cab0bfbfe9510783273799be129.png)\n\nSince $\\mathcal C$ is mapped to $\\mathcal C'$ under this transformation, the two circles are similar." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111863 "Suppose that we have an angle ![](images/06/06e47e2878930095abe73547809f3c9f87be3cd9b3b9069ca582970b60598840.svg) in the coordinate plane positioned in the usual way. Its **reference angle**, denoted ![](images/89/89ec1e1e3e3e50f78767b2b22daab75389f0186bdab3b72652b22ef5ddd2c956.svg) is the smallest positive acute angle formed with the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis. \n\nFor example, let's consider the angles ![](images/1d/1d2f5a08ccb5688494cc87753b29b5569af4bbd93c704c376f30046928f8618e.svg) and ![](images/5b/5b7ef934f94884d2eaceec6dffd5ff9fd12db70a221b3c3b3bce23b6e48efcc1.svg) These angles lie in the first, second, third, and fourth quadrants, respectively. And each of these angles has a reference angle ![](images/27/27ca7b17696552bcbf9d92c69a6ea8dac7e0e56f0f450e12dd1ad3e0a98abc6c.svg) as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfba74ff2c6a69b3db1c9478f5caac4c31578ed93b47302a74d843b04fc6ee85.png)\n\n**Note:** Any reference angle always lies between ![](images/d2/d22511f2212b05e9408f52f8da19a125897737f033bb64f480780f2f024b2091.svg) and ![](images/26/2614c435bff188ded3027f2a8f1ea011b37b41a1d62d2ff4bae181ce9107b5c2.svg) inclusively:\n\n![](images/ac/acc49c928eb9101bcc2ce3c43238a8ed33742d0eb791fca361584d556bca8b9a.svg)" #edb/ref 13194139535918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111864 "The **cotangent** is the reciprocal of the tangent:\n\n$$\n\\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}\n$$\n\nTo plot the graph of $y=\\cot x$, with $x$ in radians, we create a table with some known values:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 3$ | $\\dfrac\\pi 2$ | $\\dfrac{2\\pi} 3$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ | $\\pi$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\tan x$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ | $0$ |\n| $\\cot x$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ | $0$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ | $-1$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $\\text{undef.}$ |\n\nPlotting these points gives the following graph:\n\n![](images/e9/e9942a4d7e3da2a9da4a2e5be0cb00557562fe73685eb345995bc167eb570a49.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- a zero on the graph of $y=\\tan x$ corresponds to a vertical asymptote on the graph of $y=\\cot x$, and\n- a vertical asymptote on the graph of $y=\\tan x$ corresponds to a zero on the graph of $y=\\cot x$.\n\nThe cotangent inherits its periodicity from the tangent, so the period of the cotangent is $\\pi$. Using this periodicity property, we can extend our plot of the cotangent function:\n\n![](images/ee/ee663ad290db82185827d9c1735c57c4fdbe64b2729eaa797e65b5bbbd68fcbd.png)\n\nSome key characteristics of the function $y=\\cot x$ are:\n\n- The domain is all real numbers except at the integer multiples of $\\pi$, namely $x= 0,\\pm\\pi,\\pm 2\\pi,\\dots$\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n- The period of the function is $\\pi$.\n- There is no minimum or maximum value.\n- The zeros occur at $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots$\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points that are not in the domain: $x=0, \\pm\\pi,\\pm 2\\pi,\\dots$, and at each asymptote, the graph tends to $-\\infty$ from the left and $\\infty$ from the right." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111865 "When simplifying expressions containing $\\cot$ and $\\csc,$ we can use the following **cotangent-cosecant identity**:\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2 x= \\csc^2 x\n$$\n\nThe identity above can be obtained by starting with the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe divide both sides by $\\color{black}\\sin^2 x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}x}{{sin}^{2}x}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}x}{{sin}^{2}x} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above using $\\cot x = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}$ and $\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}$ gives\n\n$$\n1 + \\cot^2 x  = \\csc^2 x,\n$$\n\nas required." #edb/ref 13194139533439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111866 "We can rewrite the cotangent-cosecant identity in other useful forms.\n\nStarting with $1 + \\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta,$ if we subtract $1$ from both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\nThis is one alternate form of the cotangent-cosecant identity.\n\nWe can also rewrite the identity another way. Starting again with $1 + \\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta,$ if we subtract $\\cot^2\\theta$ from both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\csc^2\\theta - \\cot^2\\theta = 1.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\csc^2 \\theta - 1}.$\n\nFirst, we recognize that the denominator matches one of the identities above.\n\nSubstituting the identity into the given expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{csc}^{2}\\theta -1} & =\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}\\theta }={tan}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111867 "Recall that the coordinates of a point on the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ have the property\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf this point is in the first quadrant, we can substitute these values into the Pythagorean Identity from before to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last expression is known as the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity,** and is arguably the most important equation in trigonometry.\n\nThe Pythagorean trigonometric identity holds for *every* angle $\\theta,$ not just if $\\theta$ is in the first quadrant. In this lesson, we're focusing on the first quadrant only. We'll learn how to deal with angles in other quadrants in a future lesson." #edb/ref 13194139535913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111868 "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\sin{x}\\cos{x}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin{x} = 0,  \\qquad 0^\\circ \\leq x \\leq 180^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product rule, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\cos{x}-\\dfrac12=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{x}=0.$ The solutions to this equation that lie in the given domain are $x = 0^\\circ$ and $x=180^\\circ.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\cos{x}-\\dfrac12=0,$ or equivalently, $\\cos{x}=\\dfrac12.$ The only solution to this equation that lies in the given domain is $x = 60^\\circ.$\n\nCombining all the solutions from both equations, the solutions of the original equation (in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0^\\circ,\\quad x_2 = 60^\\circ,\\quad x_3 = 180^\\circ.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111869 "We can solve some trigonometric equations containing reciprocal functions using the zero product rule.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n \n\n$$\n(\\sec x -1)(2\\sin x +1)= 0, \\qquad -\\pi \\leq x < \\pi.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the function $\\sec{x}$ is not defined at the points where $\\cos{x}=0.$ On the interval $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ the excluded values are $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nThe equation is already factored, so by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} -1 =0, \\qquad 2\\sin{x} + 1 = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sec{x} -1 =0,$ or equivalently, $\\sec{x} =1.$ The only solution to this equation for $-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi$ is $x = 0.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\sin{x} + 1 = 0,$ or equivalently, $\\sin{x} = -\\dfrac12.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi$ are $x=-\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\nCombining all the solutions from both equations, the solutions to the original equation (in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 =  - \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},\\quad x_2 = - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},\\quad x_3 = 0.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111870 "How do we plot the graph of $y=\\arcsin x?$ As usual, we can use a table of known values.\n\nIn order to determine these known values, let's first look at the graph of the sine function.\n\n![](images/92/92dd5df32efd0c8413d7d7890cc21e8d4e76ad803491f0645b1c4ba98356055e.png)\n\nThe sine function is a one-to-one function on the interval $x\\in\\left[-\\dfrac \\pi 2, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right].$ So if we restrict the domain to this interval, we can define the inverse function $y=\\arcsin x.$\n\nRemember that the inverse swaps the domain and range of the original function. So the domain of $y=\\arcsin x$ is $x\\in[-1,1],$ while the range is $y \\in\\left[-\\dfrac \\pi 2, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right].$ In the domain, we create a table of values:\n\n| **$x$** | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{1} 2$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}} 2$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 2$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\arcsin x$ | $-\\dfrac{\\pi} 2$ | $-\\dfrac{\\pi} 3$ | $-\\dfrac{\\pi} 4$ | $-\\dfrac{\\pi} 6$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 6$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 3$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 2$ |\n\nUsing this table, we can now plot the graph of $y=\\arcsin x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/bd/bdf5638fb2a758a9cb268d7255720ec0c2b099663d118590f08949c5c7016b81.png)\n\nSome key characteristics of the graph are:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1, 1].$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in\\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}2, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right].$\n\n- The minimum value occurs at $x=-1.$\n\n- The maximum value occurs at $x=1.$\n\n- The zero occurs at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing on its entire domain." #edb/ref 13194139533439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111871 "How do we plot the graph of $y = \\arccos x?$ As usual, we can use a table of known values.\n\nIn order to determine these known values, let's first look at the graph of the cosine function.\n\n![](images/64/64d1c8ec0c1005fa73494221d859085e31bdaf1aa885b72d432ac2530034a4fe.png)\n\nThe cosine function is a one-to-one function on the interval $x\\in\\left[ 0, \\pi \\right].$ So if we restrict the domain to this interval, we can define the inverse function $y=\\arccos x.$\n\nRemember that the inverse swaps the domain and range of the original function. So the domain of $y=\\arccos x$ is $x\\in[-1,1],$ while the range is $y \\in\\left[ 0, \\pi \\right].$ In the domain, we create a table of values:\n\n| **$x$** | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{1} 2$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}} 2$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 2$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\arccos x$ | $\\pi$ | $\\dfrac{5\\pi} 6$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{2\\pi} 3$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 2$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 3$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 6$ | $0$ |\n\nNow we can use this table to plot the graph of $y=\\arccos x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/14/14a1b2786fc80b187db336c13b904b5b049907a3401efec2fe66f665c0b976b1.png)\n\nSome key characteristics of the graph are:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1, 1].$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in \\left[ 0, \\pi\\right].$\n\n- The minimum value $y=0$ occurs at $x=1.$\n\n- The maximum value $y=\\pi$ occurs at $x=-1.$\n\n- The $y$-intercept is $y=\\dfrac \\pi 2.$\n\n- The function is strictly decreasing on its entire domain." #edb/ref 13194139533439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111872 "Suppose that $P$ is a point in the first quadrant that lies on the unit circle. We can then draw a right triangle, where the hypotenuse is the line segment between the origin and the point. \n\n![](images/4b/4bae3a49b5557df9e5b50280d744aa72048b2d224df042e103a6b5d156467de7.png)\n\nSince the hypotenuse is the radius of the unit circle, its length must be equal to $1.$ And, since the triangle is a right triangle, the $(x,y)$ coordinates of any point on the unit circle in the first quadrant must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1.\n$$\n\nWe can use this to find the $y$-coordinate of a point given the $x$-coordinate, and vice-versa.\n\nFor example, suppose that the $x$-coordinate of a point $P$ in the first quadrant on the unit circle is $\\dfrac{3}{7}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=\\dfrac{3}{7}$ into the above and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t          \\left(\\dfrac{3}{7}\\right)^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{9}{49}+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= 1-\\dfrac{9}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= \\dfrac{40}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty&= \\pm\\sqrt{\\dfrac{40}{49}}\\[5pt]\n\t        y &= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}\n\n}\n$$\n\nSince $P$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive. So we select the positive answer. \n\nTherefore, our final answer is $y=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$" #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111873 "Suppose we want to plot the graph of $y=\\sin x$, where $x$ is measured in radians.\n\nFirst, let's create a table of values and include some of the values that we know:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $\\dfrac\\pi 4$ | $\\dfrac\\pi 2$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi} 4$ | $\\pi$ | $\\dfrac{5\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi} 2$ | $\\dfrac{7\\pi} 4$ | $2\\pi$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\sin x$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $0$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we get the following curve.\n\n![](images/e9/e9e6ae1bace159b05749e6749f38223de6567422e06329fcff2bbf7c0d349b1b.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we know that the function $y=\\sin x$ repeats itself every $2\\pi$ radians. So we can extend our plot using this periodicity property.\n\n![](images/1d/1dfe4444da1a8f30db08c90de8efecbe1214125b1cfbf49c2ae8681edf3d10db.png)\n\nThe key characteristics of the graph are:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- The range of the function is $y\\in[-1,1]$.\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi$.\n- Its zeros occur at $x=0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2\\pi,\\ldots$.\n- Its maximum values occur at $\\ldots,-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\ldots$\n- Its minimum values occur at $\\ldots,-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\ldots$" #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111874 "Suppose that we wish to draw the graph of the function $y=\\cos x$, with $x$ in radians.\n\nFirst, let's write up a table of values, and include some of the specific values of $\\cos x$ that we know:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $\\dfrac\\pi 4$ | $\\dfrac\\pi 2$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi} 4$ | $\\pi$ | $\\dfrac{5\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{3\\pi} 2$ | $\\dfrac{7\\pi} 4$ | $2\\pi$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\cos x$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, plotting these points, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/dd/dd9452217e387afc804ef96f3501af27c8845ec552ac4f3ae417564f14dfcd9b.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we know that $y=\\cos x$ repeats every $2\\pi$ radians. Repeating the graph using this periodicity property, we have:\n\n![](images/66/6612d11b434ccc11090d1eaab6420493035c7264a25eb6daba62f29c1821b6e3.png)\n\nSome key characteristics of the graph are:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-1,1]$.\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi$.\n- Its zeros occur at $x= \\dots,\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots$\n- Its maximum values occur at $x=0,\\pm2\\pi, \\pm4\\pi,\\dots$\n- Its minimum values occur at $x=\\pm\\pi,\\pm 3\\pi,\\dots$" #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111875 "Let's now plot the graph of $y=\\tan x$, with $x$ in radians.\n\nFirst, let's create a table for some of the values that we know:\n\n| **$x$** | $-\\dfrac\\pi 2$ | $-\\dfrac\\pi 3$ | $-\\dfrac\\pi 4$ | $-\\dfrac{\\pi} 6$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 6$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 4$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi} 3$ | $\\dfrac\\pi 2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\tan x$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $-1$ | $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt 3$ | $\\text{undefined}$ |\n\nNow, let's plot these points to estimate the graph of $y=\\tan x$.\n\n![](images/2f/2f99367baaa4b5fb6b49f6704b156f572aeba204f110b603844b7ce82bcb75a4.png)\n\nIf we inspect the unit circle carefully, we see that the function $y=\\tan x$ repeats itself every $\\pi$ radians. Therefore, we can extend our plot using this periodicity property:\n\n![](images/5b/5bf0b36273074a4794968ba2ad7bd7a95a4c1744fb2e67d81dc99a3f6ab90fb9.png)\n\nSome key characteristics of the graph of $y=\\tan x$ are as follows:\n\n- The domain of the function is almost $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ except for the points where the function is undefined, namely $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots$\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n- The period of the function is $\\pi$.\n- There is no minimum or maximum value.\n- The zeros occur at $x=0,\\pm\\pi,\\pm2\\pi,\\dots$\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points not in the domain: $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots$. At each asymptote, the graph tends to $\\infty$ from the left and $-\\infty$ from the right." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111876 "When we compute the area between a curve $y=y(x)$ and the $x$-axis using integration, we must bear the following in mind: \n\n- definite integrals that correspond to areas above the $x$-axis are positive,\n\n- definite integrals that correspond to areas below the $x$-axis are negative.\n\n![](images/db/dbbb9c009908be0bbafc58e68bb1468d9d99f984b3a8087d7ec02d0cbd2ff5db.png)\n\nThe facts above become crucial when we attempt to find the area bounded by a curve that crosses the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111877 "We can simplify the addition of two logarithms that share a base using the **product rule** for logarithms.\n\nThe **product rule** states that two logarithms which share a common base $\\color{blue}n$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}n}(a) + \\log_{\\color{blue}n}(b) = \\log_{\\color{blue}n}\\left( ab\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the product rule to rewrite $\\log_{\\color{blue}5}(4) + \\log_{\\color{blue}5}(3)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡4+{log}_{5}⁡(3) & ={log}_{5}⁡(4\\cdot 3) \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product rule works exactly the same for the natural and common logarithms." #edb/ref 13194139535905 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
